4 bugs were found. Fixed

Bug 1 — Calmar Ratio: Arithmetic annualisation → Geometric (CAGR) 

// BEFORE — **arithmetic division**. Overstates compounding, wrong for multi-year periods.
const annualizedReturn = totalReturn / yearsElapsed;

// AFTER — **geometric** (CAGR). Correct formula: (finalEquity/initialEquity)^(1/T) − 1
static calmarRatio(equityCurve, totalDays) {
  const maxDD = this.maxDrawdown(equityCurve);
  if (maxDD === 0) return Infinity;

  const initialEquity = equityCurve[0].equity;
  const finalEquity = equityCurve[equityCurve.length - 1].equity;
  if (initialEquity <= 0) return 0;

  const totalReturn = finalEquity / initialEquity;
  const yearsElapsed = Math.max(totalDays / 365, 1 / 365);

  const annualizedReturn = Math.pow(totalReturn, 1 / yearsElapsed) - 1; // CAGR

  return annualizedReturn / maxDD;
}


Impact: On a 2-year +50% backtest, arithmetic gives 25%/yr — geometric gives 22.47%. At crypto-scale returns, this diverges materially.


Bug 2 — maxDrawdownDuration: Terminal drawdown silently dropped
// BEFORE — only recorded duration when a NEW peak was reached.
// If equity curve ends still underwater, that final stretch was never counted.
for (let i = 1; i < equityCurve.length; i++) {
  if (equityCurve[i].equity >= peak) {
    const dur = i - peakIdx;
    maxDur = Math.max(maxDur, dur);
    peak = equityCurve[i].equity;
    peakIdx = i;
  }
}

// AFTER — also checks terminal drawdown after loop exits
static maxDrawdownDuration(equityCurve) {
  if (equityCurve.length < 2) return 0;

  let peak = equityCurve[0].equity;
  let peakIdx = 0;
  let maxDur = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i < equityCurve.length; i++) {
    if (equityCurve[i].equity >= peak) {
      const dur = i - peakIdx;
      maxDur = Math.max(maxDur, dur);
      peak = equityCurve[i].equity;
      peakIdx = i;
    }
  }

  // FIX: if curve ends still below last peak, count that terminal drawdown too
  const terminalDur = equityCurve.length - 1 - peakIdx;
  maxDur = Math.max(maxDur, terminalDur);

  return maxDur;
}

Impact: Any losing strategy that never recovered to its peak reported an artificially short drawdown duration — worst case was always underreported.


Bug 3 — 252 vs 365 annualisation mismatch across 3 files. (realBacktest.js already used 365 correctly)
// BEFORE — both used 252 (equities convention). Crypto trades 365 days/year.
const BARS_PER_YEAR = 252;
const dailyRf = annualRiskFree / 252;
const sharpe = (meanExcess / sigma) * Math.sqrt(252);

// AFTER — 365 everywhere, consistent across the platform
const BARS_PER_YEAR = 365;
const dailyRf = annualRiskFree / BARS_PER_YEAR;
const sharpe = (meanExcess / sigma) * Math.sqrt(BARS_PER_YEAR);

Impact: For the same strategy, Sharpe shown on the Quant Engine page was ~7% lower than the backtest engine (√365/√252 ≈ 1.203). Comparing outputs across pages gave inconsistent numbers


Bug 4 — Population variance used instead of sample variance in Sharpe
// BEFORE — biased population variance (divides by n)
function stdDev(arr) {
  const m = mean(arr);
  const variance = arr.reduce((s, x) => s + (x - m) ** 2, 0) / arr.length;
  return Math.sqrt(variance);
}

// BEFORE — same issue in store
const variance = returns.reduce((s, r) => s + Math.pow(r - mu, 2), 0) / n;

// AFTER — unbiased sample variance (n-1), consistent with realBacktest.js
function stdDev(arr) {
  const m = mean(arr);
  const variance = arr.reduce((s, x) => s + (x - m) ** 2, 0) / (arr.length - 1 || 1);
  return Math.sqrt(variance);
}

// AFTER — store
const variance = returns.reduce((s, r) => s + Math.pow(r - mu, 2), 0) / (n - 1);
const sigma = Math.sqrt(variance);
return sigma > 0 ? parseFloat(((mu / sigma) * Math.sqrt(365)).toFixed(3)) : 0;

Impact: Population variance systematically understates the true standard deviation from a sample, inflating the Sharpe ratio. Also made Bug 3 worse since the two fixes compound, annualisation factor and variance formula were wrong.

